From f82b46e666fc53b87b759fccc9d49d0813fc8ecf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 09:35:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/21] RR CPU bootstrap: the ModRaise, and the hoisted
 keyswitch family
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two blockers, both the CPU twin of GPU work already gated.

1. THE CPU ModRaise, diagnosed 2026-08-02 and never built. Every raise site wrote
   `temp = poly.GetElementAtIndex(0)` — OpenFHE's idiom for "CRT-extend a SINGLE
   residue across all towers", correct only when the bottom modulus is one prime.
   An RR chain's bottom is Cheddar's L0 = {q0, 2 tau}: three limbs, ~2^78, so
   taking tower 0 discarded two thirds of the value (observed: tower 0 carrying
   chain[1]'s modulus). New RRChain-side `RRExtendCiphertext` reconstructs the
   w-limb value per coefficient and reduces it onto the top window, CENTERED —
   the canonical representative in [0, Qw) would turn every negative coefficient
   into a number just under Qw. Same requirement the GPU gate had to honour
   against its __int128 reference. Routed at all 7 sites, IsRRChain-guarded.

   ExtendCiphertext (the COMPOSITESCALING raise) is the right shape but is Garner
   specialized to a PREFIX of compositeDegree primes, while an RR level-0 window
   sits at layout indices [14,16] — not reachable by a parameter change.

2. THE HOISTED KEYSWITCH. CoeffsToSlots does one modup per stage and many dots
   against it, accumulating in the extended basis. Milestone (b) routed
   KeySwitchCore; the hoisted family was never routed and ran the prefix
   keyswitch on RR windows, which is where both the CPU and the GPU bootstrap
   were producing garbage. Now:

   - RRChain::KeySwitchCore SPLIT into KeySwitchPrecompute / KeySwitchDotExt /
     KeySwitchDownExt (+ ExtParams), and re-expressed through them, so the fused
     and hoisted paths cannot drift apart: same digits, same positional rows,
     same ModDown.
   - Six HYBRID entry points get RR arms: KeySwitchExt, KeySwitchDown,
     KeySwitchDownFirstElement, EvalKeySwitchPrecomputeCore,
     EvalFastKeySwitchCoreExt, and EvalFastRotationExt's addFirst. The recurring
     correction is the same one: PModq and the evk rows are indexed by GLOBAL
     primeid, and window slot i is prime lo + i.
   - EvalKeySwitchPrecomputeCore is the one entry point handed params but no
     context, so GetRRChainByParams looks the chain up by crypto-params pointer
     and THROWS if it was never built — a silent fallback there would be exactly
     the corruption this routing exists to prevent. EvalBootstrap primes it.

GATE: tests/rr_cpu_gate.cpp PASS after the split and after the routing —
16.53-17.08 bits across all six windows, matching the recorded 16.46-16.76.

STATE: the CPU RR bootstrap now runs ModRaise and the whole hoisted CtS
keyswitch, and stops at `EvalMultRelin: operands must sit at the same rr level`
— level alignment before the ct*ct multiply, the CPU twin of the GPU's
adjustForAddOrSubBody. That is the next unit.

NOTE on the control: tests/rr_bts_setup_probe.cpp's `classic` mode crashes with
"double free or corruption" — but it does so IDENTICALLY on deps_n32's
pre-today OpenFHE, so it is pre-existing (its classic parameters look
under-provisioned: depth 17 at logN 13) and NOT a regression from this work. It
therefore cannot serve as the prefix-path regression check it was written to be
until its parameters are fixed.

