ENCAPS was throwing `Times(): Modulus missmatch` inside KeySwitchSparse, which
is a single-tower (q0, p) fast path: it takes tower 0 of the ciphertext, extends
it to (q, p) and returns a ONE-tower result. An RR bottom level is Cheddar's
L0 = {q0, 2 tau} — three limbs — so that path is not even well-defined there.

COMPOSITESCALING had already hit exactly this and answered it: use the standard
hybrid keyswitch for the dense->sparse direction, and generate the M-4 key with
algo->KeySwitchGen instead of KeySwitchGenSparse. RR takes the same route, for
the same reason and with the stronger one on top — the design keeps ONE key at
P*Qmax read positionally, which a single-tower helper cannot be. It is also the
CPU twin of the GPU-side createContextSwitchingKeys fix made earlier.

On the bts17 chain at dnum 5, all three secret distributions now RUN:

  uniform : 21 levels consumed, ends RR level  6, gains  5
  sparse  : 16 levels consumed, ends RR level 11, gains 10
  encaps  : 16 levels consumed, ends RR level 11, gains 10

SPARSE and ENCAPS both land on level 11 — the LAST bootstrap level — so no bare
rescale crosses the region boundary, which was the scale-convention failure.
UNIFORM's degree-88 Chebyshev still overruns by 5 levels and still crosses.

Remaining residual, shared by all three and unchanged by this commit: the output
decodes to ~1e-6 against a message of 0.5. That is the correction factor
(deg = 53 vs correctionFactor = 11), which needs a SIGNED correction and the RR
plaintext modulus, and is tracked separately.

Gates: rr_cpu_gate PASS on both chains (bts16/dnum4 and bts17/dnum5); classic
control PASS at 13.6 bits.

