From 5d149352e1372ae41dcab7e06133eac260affeed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 11:22:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 17/21] RR StC: realize the boundary-gated rescale; output now
 lands on the payload top
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

StC's plaintext scales, measured per stage, are not uniform under RR the way they
are on a classic chain:

  RR      stage 0: 2^55.4096   stage 1: 2^55.5491   stage 2: 2^39.2442
  classic stage 0: 2^27.0312   stage 1: 2^27.0306   stage 2: 2^27.0323

The odd one is not a bug: StC's LAST stage sits exactly on the bootstrap->payload
region boundary, so its plaintext carries the boundary-gated scale
sf[r-1]*F(r)/sf[r] instead of sf[r]. That is the mechanism which lands the
ciphertext on sf[r-1] AFTER a rescale — the one legitimate way to cross the
boundary, as this track established.

But the bootstrap ENDS there, so the rescale never happened and the output was
left deg-2 at a scale matching no level (2^107.18 where the level's table says
2^55.9). Realizing it — as the GPU side's `mixedChain && NoiseLevel == 2` already
does — puts the output where the design intends:

  before: RR level 11, deg 2, scale 2^107.18   (matches no level)
  after : RR level 10, deg 1, scale 2^52.00    (payload TOP; table 2^40.00)

RR level 10 is the payload top, which is exactly where a bootstrap should hand
back. SPARSE and ENCAPS both consume 17 levels and gain 9.

STILL WRONG, and it is NOT a scale error: decoding with the check bypassed
(OPENFHE_DECODE_NO_THROW=1) gives 1599.23 / 652.173 / -1164.44 against a message
of 0.5 / 0.466164 / 0.369234 — ratios 3198, 1399, -3154, inconsistent and with a
sign flip. A pure mis-scale would give one constant ratio. So StC's OUTPUT is
wrong, not merely mislabelled, and everything feeding it is verified correct.

Worth noting for whoever continues: the decode's own check is mis-parameterized
for RR the same way `deg` was — it throws when logstd > p - 5 with
p = GetPlaintextModulus() = the scalingModSize (25), giving a threshold of 20 on
a chain whose real scale is 2^40+. That is why the failure presents as
"approximation error too high" rather than as wrong numbers.

Gates: rr_cpu_gate PASS on both chains; classic control PASS at 13.6 bits.

