HAVE_INT128 indicates the compiler has __int128, not that int128_t is 128 bits --
at NATIVEINT=32 basicint.h typedefs it to int64_t while HAVE_INT128 stays defined,
so GetElementForEvalMult selected a 125-bit cap for a 64-bit word and any scalar
with operand*scFactor >= 2^63 skipped the scale-down path and wrapped silently.

Also replaces the two remaining static_cast<uint128_t>(1) << round(log2 qDouble)
with std::pow(2.0, ...), exact for a power of two and immune to the width.

